Dialogue: 0,0:00:01.50,0:00:04.88,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}I'm [Name];\NI led exploration, labels and bias.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:04.88,0:00:09.60,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}First, a fallacy check:\Nwe scanned 224 subgroup comparisons
Dialogue: 0,0:00:09.60,0:00:12.24,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}for Simpson's paradox,\Nand six reversed sign.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:13.49,0:00:17.63,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}A causal diagram suggests why:\NBMI lies between race and diabetes,
Dialogue: 0,0:00:17.63,0:00:20.56,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}so adjusting\Nfor it distorts the comparison.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:20.56,0:00:22.90,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}We report this as exploratory.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:23.30,0:00:25.62,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}Research question one: the label.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:25.62,0:00:28.99,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}Our label combines two sources,\Nand they agree substantially
Dialogue: 0,0:00:28.99,0:00:32.67,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}but far from perfectly:\NCohen's kappa is 0.70.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:33.92,0:00:40.08,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}Of 819 self-reported diabetics,\N250 test below 6.5 percent,
Dialogue: 0,0:00:40.08,0:00:42.45,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}and two thirds of those\Ntake insulin or pills.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:43.50,0:00:46.27,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}Treatment lowers\Nthe very marker we label by,
Dialogue: 0,0:00:46.27,0:00:49.90,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}so a blood-test-only label\Nwould call treated patients healthy.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:50.30,0:00:51.75,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}The other direction:
Dialogue: 0,0:00:51.75,0:00:57.07,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}152 adults have HbA1c of 6.5 or more\Nbut no diagnosis.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:58.32,0:01:00.58,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}Among adults with high HbA1c,
Dialogue: 0,0:01:00.58,0:01:05.14,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}the uninsured are 23.2 percentage points\Nmore often undiagnosed,
Dialogue: 0,0:01:05.14,0:01:09.65,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}interval 6.2 to 40.1,\Nan association from a small subgroup.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:10.90,0:01:13.60,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}So the label also records access to care.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:13.60,0:01:17.16,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}That is why we also evaluate\Non adults not yet diagnosed:
Dialogue: 0,0:01:17.16,0:01:18.90,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}the people screening is for.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:20.50,0:01:23.28,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}The leakage question,\Nfrom [Name]'s experiments.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:23.28,0:01:26.65,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}Naive preparation scores an AUC of 0.961.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:26.65,0:01:30.48,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}Minimal preparation removes\Nonly the post-diagnosis items:
Dialogue: 0,0:01:30.48,0:01:33.63,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}treatment, diagnosis history\Nand prior glucose testing.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:33.63,0:01:38.84,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}Same model, same folds: the AUC falls\Nto 0.828, a paired drop of 0.131.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:40.59,0:01:42.56,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}A deterioration we wanted:
Dialogue: 0,0:01:42.56,0:01:45.90,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}the 0.961 was the label leaking back in,\Nnot skill.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:46.30,0:01:49.87,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}[Name]'s shallow decision tree\Nshows the mechanism.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:49.87,0:01:54.97,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}Its root is a treatment item; one branch\Nthen splits on age at diagnosis.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:54.97,0:01:57.89,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}But 86 percent were never asked
Dialogue: 0,0:01:57.89,0:02:02.83,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}that question, and median imputation\Nfilled every blank with 50.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:04.08,0:02:07.90,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}So 'age 50' became a code\Nfor 'never diagnosed'.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:08.30,0:02:09.85,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}Most unexpected for me:
Dialogue: 0,0:02:09.85,0:02:14.63,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}at one shared threshold, the model flags\N70 of every 100 diagnosed diabetics,
Dialogue: 0,0:02:14.63,0:02:17.82,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}but only 52 of 100 undiagnosed ones.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:19.07,0:02:20.90,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}[Name] takes the last question.
